    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The equation for the curve for a normal distribution is",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u07-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\boldcdot e^{\\frac{-(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u07-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "where \\(\\sigma\\) is the standard deviation, \\(\\mu\\) is the mean, and \\(e\\) is a particular number close to 2.718.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u07-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Notice the part of the function that is \\((x - \\mu)\\). Using your understanding of transformations of functions, how does changing \\(\\mu\\) affect the graph of the function?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u07-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A histogram shows the number of items in the data set that fall into specified intervals. A relative frequency histogram shows the proportion of the entire data set that falls into specified intervals.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u07-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-033",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, a study measured the handspan of 1,000 adults in centimeters. A handspan is the distance from the thumb to the smallest finger when the fingers are stretched out as much as possible. A histogram shows the number of people…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u07-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U07-L05-ACTIVITY-034",
      "SourceSection": "5.4: The Normal Curve",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A histogram that uses the relative frequencies shows a distribution with the same shape, but the heights of the bars represent the relative frequency for the corresponding intervals. For example, out of all the adults in the study, 13.2%…",
